    "id": "q40",
    "sourceQuestionNo": 40,
    "stemEn": "Match List - I with List - II.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:\n\n(a) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)\n\n(b) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)\n\n(c) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)\n\n(d) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)\n\n41. In an experiment, a student in the laboratory measures the terminal potential difference (V) of a cell (emf E and internal resistance r) as a function of current I flowing through it and plot a graph taking V on y-axis and I on x-axis. What is the slope of the graph between V and I?",
    "optionsEn": [
     {
      "id": "A",
      "text": "$ r^{2} $"
     },
     {
      "id": "B",
      "text": "$ -r^{2} $"
     },
     {
      "id": "C",
      "text": "r"
     },
     {
      "id": "D",
      "text": "r Compare it with standard equation of the line: $ y = mx + c $ $ \\Rightarrow y = c + mx $ Since graph of student is V vs I $ \\therefore $ V is y and I is x Since graph of student is $V$ vs $1$ : $V$ is $y$ and $I$ is $x$ Therefore, $(-r)$ is slope"
     }
